            <ArticleTitle>A double-blind randomized control study to determine the effect of Visthesia viscoelastic substance on pain following LASEK.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>229-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1159/000130071</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the efficacy of topical Visthesia drops and viscoelastic in reducing pain during the early postoperative period following laser-assisted subepithelial keratectomy (LASEK).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In a prospective randomized double-blind study, LASEK was performed on both eyes of patients using the Technolas 217Z Laser (Bausch &amp; Lomb). Visthesia was placed in one eye and placebo (Healon) in the fellow eye, following insertion of a bandage contact lens on completion of surgery. Pain experienced on the evening of surgery and the following morning were scored using a visual analogue scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">18 patients of mean age 27.3 years were examined. Mean refractive spherical equivalent was not statistically different between both groups (p = 0.6). No statistically significant difference (p = 0.87) in pain was detected between both groups on the evening of surgery or the following morning (p = 0.12).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Visthesia, applied topically on completion of LASEK, did not have a significant effect on pain during the early postoperative period compared with placebo.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>(c) 2008 S. Karger AG, Basel.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
